every year sciences reporters and  editors  choose nine runners-up and one  breakthrough of the year but before we  get to the breakthrough  here are a few of the runners up  fast radio bursts short powerful flashes  of radio waves from distant galaxies  have tantalized astronomers for 13 years  now cosmic detectives have figured out  where they come from  magnetars neutron stars that  fizzle and pop with powerful magnetic  fields  in april an frb went off in the milky  way  researchers quickly narrowed the source  to a small area of sky  and using powerful telescopes they  quickly linked it to a single magnetar  stay tuned for a sequel as researchers  try to understand exactly how the  magnetars produce these radio bursts  since the revolutionary gene editing  tool  known as crispr burst onto the scene in  2012  it's given researchers new power to  engineer crops and animals  stirred ethical debates and earned a  nobel prize  now it scored its first victory in a  clinical trial  it helped patients with two inherited  blood diseases  beta thalassemia where low levels of an  oxygen carrying protein  called hemoglobin lead to weakness and  exhaustion  and sickle cell disease caused by a  defective form of the same protein  to treat sickle cell disease researchers  used crispr  to make the body produce the fetal form  of hemoglobin  but the crispr approach could cost one  million dollars  or more per patient putting it out of  reach for many  their eyes are beaty and their brains no  bigger than a walnut  but two studies published this year  suggest birds  have amazing mental powers scientists  took a closer look at the forebrain of  homing pigeons and owls  and found nerves there that connect  horizontally  and vertically this is similar to the  matrix of connections  seen in the human neocortex the source  of our  occasionally incredible intelligence  another study suggested that carrion  crows  already well known for their brains are  aware  of what they see and do this could mean  that some form of consciousness was  present  in our last common ancestor 320  million years ago  more than 40 000 years ago on the  indonesian island of sulawesi a  prehistoric picasso  ventured deep into a cave and sketched a  series of fantastic  animal-headed hunters cornering wild  hogs and buffalos  it's possible that the artist was  depicting the hunters  wearing masks or camouflage but they may  also  represent mythical animal human hybrids  which appear in other ancient works of  art  the age of the paintings makes them the  earliest known  figurative art made by modern humans  and now the breakthrough of the year  not one not two but  multiple vaccines have  now succeeded in large human clinical  trials  a mere 11 months after the first  sequencing of sars cov2  vaccines have already been deployed in  the uae the united kingdom  and now the united states the two  vaccines at the forefront of this wave  which use messenger rna to carry the  genetic code for the coronavirus spike  protein  reported an amazing efficacy roughly  95 but other approaches  like using inactivated cold viruses to  shuttle the spike protein  or harmless versions of the virus itself  could  also soon make their way to market  never before have so many competitors  collaborated  so openly and never before have  governments  businesses and institutions all around  the world  poured this much money and manpower  into the hunt for a vaccine  but even as this research has yielded  remarkable results  the pandemic also marks a breakdown in  communication  public trust and a sense of shared  reality  that has transformed the deadly threat  of coven 19  into something far worse  as of mid-december more than 1.6 million  people  have died globally of complications from  coven 19  with more than 300 000 deaths in the  united states alone  science has delivered an exit strategy  through effective vaccines but human  behavior has been  and continues to be the most important  variable  in shaping our path forward  [Music]
 

